A1. Out of the marriage ceremony. ~ OBEY
A5. The last of the stogies. ~ STUBS
A10. A woman's all-day visit. ~ CHAT
A14. Here's where you come to the ball on a horse. ~ POLO
A15. A violence. ~ THROE
A16. A choker. ~ ROPE
A17. A real woe word. ~ ALAS
A18. Queen Mary is like this. ~ REGAL
A19. Our parents saw this lady in "Lohengrin." ~ ELSA
A20. Blue. ~ LONESOME
A22. How the crook pays his income tax. ~ EVADES
A24. It's difficult to discover one in a beauty parlor. ~ MALE
A25. Cats know this word. ~ SCAT
A26. The younger generation is hunting this. ~ THRILL
A29. You better cut this out (plural). ~ PATTERNS
A33. This is the way to go home. ~ ROUTE
A34. What the queen does to the jack. ~ TAKES
A35. Caught at home. ~ OUT
A36. This came up out of the sea. ~ ISLE
A37. Gave a wordy recital. ~ RAVED
A38. This lad knows his movies. ~ PARE
A39. What any duffer can do to a golf ball. ~ TEE
A40. Take pains with these. ~ PANES
A41. Coolidge is this. ~ TERSE
A42. Often used up before they are gotten. ~ EARNINGS
A44. Completely washed out. ~ RINSED
A45. School children have these on their hands. ~ INKS
A46. The terrible part of a saxophone. ~ REED
A47. One thing to do with your brains if they don't work. ~ CUDGEL
A50. These go together. ~ COMRADES
A54. Brigham Young's state. ~ UTAH
A55. Descriptive of the father in the movies. ~ IRATE
A57. Back of the neck. ~ NAPE
A58. Might be Hoover's nickname. ~ BERT
A59. One's birth. ~ NATAL
A60. Faw' down. ~ TRIP
A61. A man does this to get a wife. ~ ASKS
A62. You can see through this one. ~ GHOST
A63. A faction. ~ SECT

D1. A semi-precious stone. ~ OPAL
D2. A foreign weapon. ~ BOLO
D3. A dash—but not for liberty. ~ ELAN
D4. A valley in California. ~ YOSEMITE
D5. You can always be seen taking this down the street. ~ STROLL
D6. This is not always a pleasant subject. ~ THEME
D7. How to get that new dress. ~ URGE
D8. Its embraces simply kill you. ~ BOA
D9. The winner of a beauty contest is this. ~ SELECTED
D10. This is what fashion experts do. ~ CREATE
D11. What the wrestler lost on his opponent. ~ HOLD
D12. Part of a church. ~ APSE
D13. Cocktail parties. ~ TEAS
D21. You'll always see this after a fire. ~ SALE
D23. These can certainly hold their beer. ~ VATS
D25. An expression that grandma uses. ~ SAKES
D26. Worn out. ~ TRITE
D27. A prophet. ~ HOSEA
D28. You can cover your yard with this. ~ RULER
D29. Prepares the way for an automobile accident. ~ PAVES
D30. These come from Bellows Falls. ~ ROARS
D31. She'll fix your neck and break your heart. ~ NURSE
D32. This animal can eat when it hasn't a bit in its mouth. ~ STEED
D34. This used to come in packages of cigarettes. ~ TANGS
D37. This kind of a pain rankles. ~ RANKLING
D38. These had to swing because of a rich woman. ~ PENDANTS
D40. This little fellow wants a cone. ~ PINE
D41. This is a row for the shore. ~ TIER
D43. These are long in winter. ~ NIGHTS
D44. Liquefy again. ~ REMELT
D46. Rosters. ~ ROTAS
D47. They used to have rough-riders here. ~ CUBA
D48. Indians. ~ UTES
D49. Keep your wife in this if she's inquisitive. ~ DARK
D50. A Roman historian. ~ CATO
D51. What the coward wouldn't take. ~ DARE
D52. An heroic narrative. ~ EPIC
